\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {The paper in one figure: the statistic moves, admission does not, and suppression holds.} Mode~S, Krum under model-scaling (\S  \ref {sec:targeted}). Every adversary is pinned at $c{=}1$, so the adversarial coefficient share is constant across rungs, Lemma~\ref {lem:annihilation}'s attenuation channel is shut \emph  {by construction}, and the dose \textbf  {isolates statistic disturbance from adversarial coefficient attenuation}---the one channel it closes, and not the only thing it moves. As benign dispersion $\rho $ grows to $54.6$, Krum's \emph  {decision} flips in up to $80\%$ of rounds while \emph  {admission} of adversarial input holds at exactly $0.000$ and max-committed ASR shows no evidence of the predicted increase, staying inside the pre-specified equivalence margin ($|\Delta |{=}0.026 < 0.15$) and low. \textbf  {Statistic preservation is therefore not what was doing the work.} Bars are Student-$t$ $95\%$ intervals ($n{=}5$); every value recomputed per seed by \texttt  {analyze\_targeted\_dose.py}. A pre-registered replication in a \emph  {different} invariance class and on a \emph  {second dataset and architecture} reproduces the negative and \textbf  {refutes} the cross-arm admission ordering we had predicted (\S  \ref {sec:targeted_rest}).}}{2}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:modeS}{{1}{2}{\textbf {The paper in one figure: the statistic moves, admission does not, and suppression holds.} Mode~S, Krum under model-scaling (\S \ref {sec:targeted}). Every adversary is pinned at $c{=}1$, so the adversarial coefficient share is constant across rungs, Lemma~\ref {lem:annihilation}'s attenuation channel is shut \emph {by construction}, and the dose \textbf {isolates statistic disturbance from adversarial coefficient attenuation}---the one channel it closes, and not the only thing it moves. As benign dispersion $\rho $ grows to $54.6$, Krum's \emph {decision} flips in up to $80\%$ of rounds while \emph {admission} of adversarial input holds at exactly $0.000$ and max-committed ASR shows no evidence of the predicted increase, staying inside the pre-specified equivalence margin ($|\Delta |{=}0.026 < 0.15$) and low. \textbf {Statistic preservation is therefore not what was doing the work.} Bars are Student-$t$ $95\%$ intervals ($n{=}5$); every value recomputed per seed by \texttt {analyze\_targeted\_dose.py}. A pre-registered replication in a \emph {different} invariance class and on a \emph {second dataset and architecture} reproduces the negative and \textbf {refutes} the cross-arm admission ordering we had predicted (\S \ref {sec:targeted_rest})}{figure.1}{}}
